            %p In total, these three lifting conditions are precisely those for a Grothendieck fibration in groupoids.
          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Under the operation of forming the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+quasi-category"} opposite quasi-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to D
              is a left fibration then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mi op
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{op} : C^{op} \to D^{op}
              is a right fibration.
            %p Therefore it is sufficient to list properties of only one type of these fibrations, that for the other follows.
          %h3#homotopy_lifting_property Homotopy lifting property
          %p
            In classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : E \to B
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is said to have the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+lifting+property"} homotopy lifting property
            if it has the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+lifting+property"} right lifting property
            with respect to all morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Id
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \stackrel{(Id, 0)}{\to} Y \times I
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = [0,1]
            the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            and every commuting diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
