            %em uniquely
            \. This is the situation that the following proposition generalizes:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : X \to S
              of simplicial sets is a left fibration precisely if the canonical morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X^{\Delta[1]} \to X^{\{0\}} \times_{S^{\{0\}}} S^{\Delta^1}
            %p is a trivial Kan fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is a corollary of the characterization of left anodyne morphisms in
              %a{:href => "#PropRightAnodyne"} Properties of left anodyne maps
              by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Andre Joyal
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, corollary 2.1.2.10
          %p
            By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law+for+pullbacks"} pasting law for pullbacks
            this implies
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : X \to S
              be a left fibration of simplicial sets, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : Y \to X
              be a morphism of simplicial sets. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a left fibration iff
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p f
              is a left fibration
          %h3#AsFibrationsInInfinityGroupoids
            As fibrations in
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The notion of right fibration of quasi-categories generalizes the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+fibered+in+groupoids"} category fibered in groupoids
          #OverKanComplex.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \to T
              are automatically
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This appears as
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop. 2.1.3.3
          %p As an important special case:
          %p
          .num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to *
              a right (left) fibration over the
